─── Response ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Great! I've successfully reimplemented the C++ solveTask method in Haskell.
Here's what I've created:


                          Summary of Implementation

✅ ClientResults Module (src/ClientResults.hs)

 • RunResult data type for individual program results
 • ClientResults data type tracking best results for each program length
   (1-16)
 • Functions: newClientResults, addResult, mergeResults, getTask,
   getLongestProgram, getLongestCharactersPrinted

✅ PackProcessor Module (src/PackProcessor.hs)

 • Device serialization functions for packing/unpacking programs into
   32/64-bit words
 • Functions: packHalfDevice, unpackHalfDevice, packDevice, unpackDevice

✅ SolveTask Module (src/SolveTask.hs)

 • Main solveTask function that mirrors the C++ logic
 • Generates program variations by incrementing memory locations starting from
   position 8
 • Tests each variation and tracks the best results for each program length
 • Includes debug printing capability

The implementation faithfully recreates the C++ algorithm:

 1 Unpacks the task into an initial program
 2 Uses nested loop logic to generate program variations
 3 Tests each program with runProgramLoopCheck
 4 Tracks the best results (most characters printed) for each program length
 5 Returns a ClientResults object containing all the best programs found

The Haskell version uses immutable data structures and functional programming
patterns while maintaining the same algorithmic behavior as the original C++
code.

Would you like me to:

 1 Test the implementation with some sample tasks?
 2 Add any additional functionality?
 3 Optimize or refactor any part of the code?
 4 Create integration tests?
