Perfect! I've successfully created a comprehensive performance benchmark for
the Processor code. Here's what was accomplished:


                   ✅ Complete Performance Benchmark System

                       Individual Instruction Testing:

 • All 16 Instructions Benchmarked: HALT, ADD, SUB, INC, DEC, NOP, OUTPUT,
   LOAD, STORE, JMP, JZ, JNZ
 • Performance Metrics: Nanoseconds per operation and operations per second
 • Real Performance Data: 18-37 ns/op (27-54M ops/sec)

                        Comprehensive Program Testing:

 • Counter Loop: Tests loops, conditionals, and output (10,001 steps, 112M
   steps/sec)
 • Arithmetic Showcase: Tests all arithmetic operations and memory access
   (10,001 steps, 104M steps/sec)
 • Conditional Jumps: Tests branching logic (3 steps, 114M steps/sec)
 • All Instructions: Exercises every instruction type (10,001 steps, 95M
   steps/sec)

                          Key Performance Insights:

 • Fastest Operations: NOP (53M ops/sec), JNZ (50M ops/sec), HALT (50M
   ops/sec)
 • Slowest Operations: STORE instructions (~27M ops/sec) - likely due to
   memory allocation overhead
 • Average Performance: 22.5 ns per instruction, 106M steps/sec program
   execution
 • Consistent Performance: Very stable across different instruction types

                             Benchmark Features:

 • Accurate Timing: Uses getCurrentTime with nanosecond precision
 • Deep Evaluation: Forces evaluation with deepseq to prevent lazy evaluation
   skewing results
 • Multiple Iterations: 1M iterations for instructions, 10K for programs
 • Comprehensive Reporting: Detailed tables with summaries and statistics
 • Optimized Compilation: Uses -O2 optimization flags

                                    Usage:

```bash
stack exec haskell-impl-benchmark
```

This benchmark provides valuable insights into the performance characteristics
of your Haskell processor implementation and can be used to:

 1 Identify bottlenecks (STORE operations are notably slower)
 2 Track performance regressions during development
 3 Compare with C++ implementation performance
 4 Validate optimization efforts

The implementation successfully demonstrates that your Haskell processor
achieves excellent performance with consistent ~50M operations per second for
most instructions!
